6. One test read closely

  test_add_math_case_with_unverified_verdicts_refused (eaf1bff,
  test_casememory.py lines 75-95).
  Left: nothing. Right: self.assertRaises(CaseVerificationError) around
  store.add(...) for each of four evidence dicts, then
  assertEqual(len(store.all()), 0).
  Where each side comes from: the four evidence dicts are hand-written in the
  test; the store is read back from disk. The store read is a real oracle (the
  record must not exist). The weak side is the exception type: four dicts that
  differ from a storable case in several ways at once cannot say which validation
  refused them. Section 2 shows the cost of that directly.
  A stronger form needs no new oracle: give each unverified verdict a complete,
  otherwise-valid evidence payload (points_tested >= 1, finite
  max_relative_error), so the verdict is the only thing that can refuse it.

